# /mcp status command, deterministic and LLM-free. Proves the .mcp.json
# auto-merge, the /mcp command path, and the zag.detach status worker survive
# end to end without crashing the TUI.
#
# Setup (via the parameterized launcher): a fixture HOME plus a project dir
# whose .mcp.json declares a SECOND server (jsonsrv, standard command-string +
# args shape) alongside the Lua-declared `fake`. The launcher cd's into that
# project dir before exec'ing zag, so zag's cwd-relative .mcp.json read merges
# jsonsrv on the first coroutine-context config load (which /mcp triggers).
#
# WHAT THIS SCENARIO CAN ASSERT ON THE GRID: only that the command ran and the
# TUI survived. zag.notify (the channel /mcp uses for its status text) routes
# to the file log, NOT the conversation grid -- there is no grid-visible
# rendering of notify output today (LuaEngine.zagNotifyFn writes to the
# .lua_user log scope; a future phase will push it to a compositor toast). The
# probe-verified grid stays on the welcome screen after /mcp. So a wait_text on
# "MCP: ", "jsonsrv", or "fake" against the grid would always time out, NOT
# because the plumbing is broken but because the text never reaches the grid.
#
# WHERE THE STATUS TEXT ACTUALLY LANDS: the fixture log under
# $ZAG_SIM_MCP_HOME/.zag/logs/<uuid>.log, as a line like
#   [lua_user] info: [notify] MCP: 0/2 servers, 0 tools
#   ○ fake (not connected)
#   ○ jsonsrv (not connected)
# The header N/2 count and both server names (fake from config.lua, jsonsrv
# from .mcp.json) are asserted by inspecting that log post-run. The fixture
# HOME is a fixed path so the log is findable.
#
# A /mcp that wedged the detach worker, crashed on the .mcp.json merge, or
# left the TUI unresponsive would surface here as a non-clean exit (the final
# /quit + wait_exit would time out or report a crash).

set env ZAG_SIM_MCP_HOME=/tmp/zag-sim-mcp-status-home
set env ZAG_SIM_MCP_PROJECT_DIR=/tmp/zag-sim-mcp-status-proj
spawn ./src/sim/scenarios/testdata/mcp_launch.sh
wait_text /Welcome to zag/
wait_idle 500ms
send "/mcp" <Enter>
# /mcp routes through a zag.detach worker; give it a moment to run and notify.
# We can't anchor on the status text (see header), so settle on idle instead.
wait_idle 2s 8s
snapshot after_mcp
send "/quit" <Enter>
wait_exit
